To create a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text, you can use a library like `markdowncpp`. First, ensure you have included the necessary headers and linked the `markdowncpp` library. Here is a simple implementation:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <markdowncpp/markdown.h>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdownText) {
    markdowncpp::Markdown md;
    std::string htmlText = md.convert(markdownText);
    return htmlText;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdownText = "# Heading\n\nThis is a paragraph.";
    std::string htmlText = markdownToHtml(markdownText);
    std::cout << "Markdown: " << markdownText << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML: " << htmlText << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses the `markdowncpp::Markdown` class to convert the markdown text to HTML. The `convert` method of the `Markdown` class processes the markdown string and returns the corresponding HTML string. The `main` function demonstrates how to use the `markdownToHtml` function by printing both the markdown and HTML formatted text to the console.

